{"id":"h213","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"The 13C spectrum of which isomer of C6H14 has lines with five distinct chemical shifts?\nIs the correct answer \"hexane\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Hexane is not the isomer whose 13C spectrum shows five lines.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"n-Hexane has mirror symmetry so C1≡C6, C2≡C5, C3≡C4.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"n-Hexane gives 3 distinct 13C signals, not 5.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"2-Methylpentane has six inequivalent carbons and gives 6 signals.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"3-Methylpentane gives 4 signals.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"2,2-Dimethylbutane gives 4 signals.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"2,3-Dimethylbutane gives 2 signals.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"No C6H14 isomer gives exactly five distinct 13C shifts, so the question's premise fails.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"One might naively assume the straight-chain isomer is the one with five shifts (a weak case for hexane).","confidence":0.1,"lean":1,"theta":0.1571,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":7,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":8,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":7,"j":8,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"}],"readout_weights":[0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111,0.1111],"stages":[{"stage":"encode","qubits":[{"amp":0.9989,"phase":-3.1416,"p_yes":0.0006,"bloch":[-0.9989,-0.